<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Licchavi Kingdom of Nepal</h1><p class="st-meta">400 CE – 750 CE (approx.) · capital Kathmandu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first historically documented kingdom of the Kathmandu Valley, whose Sanskrit inscriptions and temples laid the foundations of Nepalese culture.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavis</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Nepal</a> claimed descent from the ancient Licchavi clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishali">Vaishali</a>, part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vajji">Vajji</a> league in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. They were established in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a> by the 5th century, succeeding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kirata-dynasty">Kirata dynasty</a> of tradition. The earliest dated inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, set up by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manadeva-i">Manadeva I</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changu-narayan-temple">Changu Narayan</a> in 464, records his military campaigns.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavi</a> kings ruled through officials and village councils, and their many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> inscriptions record land grants, water channels and temple endowments. Both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaiva</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashupatinath-temple">Pashupatinath</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> flourished. In the early 7th century the powerful minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amshuvarman">Amshuvarman</a> took royal titles and built the Kailashakuta palace. The valley grew rich as a staging post on the trade route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narendradeva">Narendradeva</a>, restored with Tibetan help, the kingdom acknowledged Tibetan influence. Inscriptions thin out after the mid-8th century, and the valley's history becomes obscure until the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thakuri-dynasty">Thakuri dynasty</a> appears in 879. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal-sambat">Nepal Sambat</a> era of 879 marks that new beginning.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/licchavi-kingdom-of-nepal.jpg" alt="Licchavi Kingdom of Nepal" width="320" height="165"><figcaption>Image: Smith, Vincent Arthur, 1848-1920Edwardes, S. M. (Stephen Meredyth), 1873-1927 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amshuverma_of_Nepal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>400 CE – 750 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kathmandu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Manadeva I, Amshuvarman, Narendradeva</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Licchavis of Nepal, Licchavi dynasty (Nepal)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=575&amp;focus=licchavi-kingdom-of-nepal" aria-label="Licchavi Kingdom of Nepal on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6919 1162 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Licchavi Kingdom of Nepal lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7239 1418L7362 1550M7239 1418L7239 1418"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7362 1550h0M7239 1418h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7239 1418h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7239 1418h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kathmandu. <a href="/atlas?year=575&amp;focus=licchavi-kingdom-of-nepal">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
